WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.194
A model of three hundred and seventy-six gigabytes, answering on a Mac

00:00:04.194 --> 00:00:06.378
with a fraction of that in memory.

00:00:06.758 --> 00:00:10.433
The rest stays on the SSD, and gets read as each token needs it.

00:00:12.623 --> 00:00:13.435
Hi, I'm Hollis.

00:00:13.435 --> 00:00:15.329
Two engines did this the same week.

00:00:15.329 --> 00:00:16.953
Here is how the reads work.

00:00:20.725 --> 00:00:25.865
A token of Qwen three point six uses eight of a layer's experts.

00:00:25.865 --> 00:00:30.610
InferSSD indexes each one's bytes inside the ordinary GGUF, and copies nothing.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:34.544
Reads go through one ring, with direct I/O, so the page cache

00:00:34.544 --> 00:00:36.017
never keeps a second copy.

00:00:36.417 --> 00:00:39.831
They land in a RAM cache whose size comes from the memory

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:41.253
budget, and frequent experts stay.

00:00:41.653 --> 00:00:44.168
And the CPU multiplies the expert right there.

00:00:44.168 --> 00:00:48.254
One token is one row, so moving it to a GPU buys little.

00:00:50.158 --> 00:00:53.471
Done naively, the CPU waits for every read, and the drive

00:00:53.471 --> 00:00:54.675
waits for the router.

00:00:55.158 --> 00:00:57.599
So every gate and up read goes first.

00:00:57.599 --> 00:01:01.259
Half of each expert's math starts while the down matrices still stream.

00:01:01.679 --> 00:01:03.693
Cached experts need no read at all.

00:01:03.693 --> 00:01:07.433
But now the drive sits idle while the GPU runs the next layer.

00:01:07.853 --> 00:01:11.295
So the next layer's router runs early on this layer's input, and

00:01:11.295 --> 00:01:13.876
the drive reads its likely picks into that gap.

00:01:15.858 --> 00:01:16.982
Here is the formula.

00:01:17.858 --> 00:01:19.607
Each speculative read is priced first.

00:01:19.607 --> 00:01:22.523
P is the chance the next layer really uses it.

00:01:22.943 --> 00:01:25.544
R is the share still unread when the gap closes.

00:01:25.544 --> 00:01:27.886
A weak guess on a long read gets skipped.

00:01:29.392 --> 00:01:32.816
Local Code replayed real conversations on a DeepSeek model.

00:01:32.816 --> 00:01:38.142
With twenty-four gibibytes for experts, eighty-two lookups in a hundred never touch the drive.

00:01:38.562 --> 00:01:39.920
At forty, ninety-four do.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.673
Routing is skewed, so a small cache holds most of what a conversation uses.

00:01:46.425 --> 00:01:50.532
On DeepSeek the README reports about seven tokens a second on an

00:01:50.532 --> 00:01:54.297
RTX five-oh-nine-oh, against under one for llama dot c p p.

00:01:54.297 --> 00:01:57.035
It publishes no number for either Qwen model.

00:01:58.575 --> 00:02:01.690
Local Code does the same inside a Mac app.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.420
The post says thirty-two gigabytes.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.919
Its demo ran at a forty-eight gigabyte setting, on a sixty-four gigabyte Mac.

00:02:09.142 --> 00:02:10.672
Streaming makes the model run.

00:02:10.672 --> 00:02:14.037
The hit rate decides how fast, and neither post measured it.

00:02:16.525 --> 00:02:19.493
Experts on the drive, reads in the right order, and guesses

00:02:19.493 --> 00:02:20.573
read into the gap.

00:02:21.125 --> 00:02:23.200
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:23.792
I'm Hollis.

00:02:23.792 --> 00:02:24.089
Bye!
